Recruitment Funnel Analysis
Business Analyst Portfolio Project  ·  Anannya Anesweta Rout  ·  2024

Executive Summary
This analysis examines 300 candidate records across 6 departments to identify inefficiencies in the hiring funnel. Key findings reveal that Employee Referrals produce 4x better hire rates than LinkedIn, that 36% of candidates drop off before screening, and that Marketing takes the longest to hire at 42 days on average. Three actionable recommendations are presented to improve hiring efficiency.
Key Metrics at a Glance
Metric	Value
Total applicants analysed	300
Overall hire rate	13.7%
Offer acceptance rate	82.0%
Average time to hire	39 days
Best performing source	Employee Referral (32.8% hire rate)
Fastest hiring department	Finance & Sales (37 days avg)
Biggest funnel drop	Applied → Screened (36.3% stuck at applied)
1. Funnel Drop-off Analysis
The funnel reveals a significant early drop-off problem. Over one-third of candidates (36.3%) never progress past the application stage, suggesting the screening process or job description clarity may need improvement.
Stage	Candidates	% of Total
Applied	109	36.3%
Screened	78	26.0%
Interview Round 1	39	13.0%
Interview Round 2	23	7.7%
HR Round	1	0.3%
Offer Made	9	3.0%
Hired	41	13.7%
Key Insight
•	The biggest drop happens at the very top of the funnel — 36% of applicants are rejected or ghost after applying
•	Only 26% pass screening, suggesting job descriptions may be attracting poorly matched candidates
•	Offer acceptance is strong at 82% — once candidates reach offer stage, they are likely to accept
2. Recruitment Source Effectiveness
Employee Referrals are by far the most effective source, producing a 32.8% hire rate — more than double any other channel. LinkedIn, despite generating the most applications, has the lowest hire rate at 7.8%.
Source	Applicants	Hired	Hire Rate	Avg Days to Hire
Employee Referral	58	19	32.8%	38.7
Naukri	68	8	11.8%	34.0
Instahyre	17	2	11.8%	40.0
Company Website	33	3	9.1%	43.0
LinkedIn	90	7	7.8%	39.7
Campus Drive	34	2	5.9%	44.0
Key Insight
•	Highest hire rate (32.8%) with competitive time-to-hire — the most cost-efficient sourceEmployee Referral: 
•	Most applicants (90) but worst conversion (7.8%) — high volume, low qualityLinkedIn: 
•	Lowest hire rate (5.9%) and slowest to hire (44 days) — ROI needs reviewCampus Drive: 
3. Department Performance
Finance and Engineering lead in hire rates, while Operations struggles the most at 9.1%. Marketing takes the longest to fill positions at 42 days average.
Department	Applicants	Hired	Hire Rate	Avg Days to Hire
Finance	28	5	17.9%	37
Engineering	91	15	16.5%	40
Sales	74	10	13.5%	37
HR	23	3	13.0%	39
Marketing	29	3	10.3%	42
Operations	55	5	9.1%	37
4. Why Are Candidates Dropping Out?
Rejection reasons are almost evenly distributed, suggesting no single dominant problem. However, 'No show' (21.2%) and 'Withdrew' (17.8%) together account for nearly 39% of rejections — indicating candidate engagement and communication issues.
Rejection Reason	Count	% of Rejections
No show	55	21.2%
Salary expectation mismatch	53	20.5%
Better candidate selected	53	20.5%
Skills mismatch	52	20.1%
Candidate withdrew	46	17.8%
5. Recommendations
Recommendation 1 — Double down on Employee Referrals
Employee Referrals have a 32.8% hire rate vs 7.8% for LinkedIn. The company should introduce a formal referral incentive programme (cash bonus or recognition) to increase referral volume from the current 19% of applications to 30%+.
Expected impact: If referrals increase from 58 to 90 applications, estimated additional hires: +10 per year.
Recommendation 2 — Fix the top-of-funnel drop-off
36% of candidates drop at the application stage. This indicates job descriptions may be unclear or overly generic, attracting mismatched applicants. Recommended actions:
•	Rewrite JDs with specific skill requirements and clear salary bands
•	Add a short pre-screening questionnaire (3–5 questions) to filter early
•	Target: reduce early drop-off from 36% to under 20%
Recommendation 3 — Reduce time-to-hire in Marketing
Marketing takes 42 days on average — 5 days longer than the fastest departments. With only 3 hires from 29 applicants, the process appears bottlenecked. Recommended actions:
•	Audit the Marketing interview process — identify which round causes most delay
•	Set an SLA: all interview rounds completed within 21 days of application
•	Consider building a pre-qualified talent pool for Marketing roles
6. Methodology & Tools
•	Dataset: 300 synthetic recruitment records generated to reflect realistic hiring patterns
•	Analysis: SQL queries run in SQLite covering funnel, source, department, time-to-hire, and rejection analysis
•	Visualisation: Power BI dashboard (see attached .pbix file)
•	Documentation: Business findings written as a BA deliverable, not a technical report


